  "root_cause_analysis": "The model treated the initial flight-cost query as informational rather than recognizing that user intent to book was explicit ('could you make the booking'). This caused a one-turn delay in invoking book_flight. The airport resolution redundancy stemmed from over-cautious parameter validation rather than trusting the user's city names for initial lookup.",
  "correct_approach": "In Turn 0, invoke get_flight_cost with user-provided cities (NYC/Tokyo), then immediately chain book_flight with the same access_token and card_id without awaiting a separate confirmation turn. Avoid redundant airport calls; resolve once per route and reuse codes. For Twitter, invoke authenticate_twitter only if required by the tool; post_tweet and retweet in sequence using returned tweet ID.",
  "key_insight": "State-modifying calls (book_flight, post_tweet, retweet) must execute immediately when user intent and required parameters are present, not deferred to subsequent turns. Redundant lookups and verbose narratives do not break evaluation if the core tool sequence and parameters remain valid."
}

